=====Usage notes=====
In traditional standard English {{m|en||as}} (like {{m|en|than}}) is a conjunction, not a preposition. The use of pronominal case forms (subject vs. object) therefore depends on the syntactical context. Compare:
* {{ux|en|She loves you just as much as '''I''' [do].}}
* {{ux|en|She loves you just as much as [she loves] '''me'''.}}
In modern everyday English, this difference may be lost and the use of bare subject forms (''I, he, she, we, they'') after {{m|en||as}} may seem pedantic. Only the object forms are used on their own.
* {{ux|en|You are not as tall as '''I'''. ''(formal/dated short form)''}}
* {{ux|en|You are not as tall as '''me'''. ''(informal short form)''}}
* {{ux|en|You are not as tall as '''I am'''. ''(full form)''}}

